INT. HOUSEBOAT GALLEY - CHRISTMAS EVE - NIGHT

A tiny kitchen rocking gently on a canal. Steam on the window. A pot of tomato sauce. Strings of paper lanterns.

An OLD MAN, 80s, a cardigan, a wooden spoon, stirs the pot with a trembling hand. He hums something Neapolitan.

CHIARA, 40s, in a coat, steps through the hatch, shaking rain off. She stops. Looks at him.

CHIARA
Papa, you're not supposed to be cooking.

OLD MAN
I'm not cooking. I'm supervising.

CHIARA
The sauce is boiling.

OLD MAN
It is supposed to boil.

She crosses and takes the spoon. He lets her. She stirs, tastes, smiles.

CHIARA
Salt.

OLD MAN
It has salt.

CHIARA
More.

OLD MAN
Chiara, I made this sauce before you could walk.

CHIARA
I know. That's how I know it needs more.

LUCA, 12, her son, bursts in carrying a plastic reindeer. He wears a Santa hat two sizes too big.

LUCA
Mamma! Nonno! I found the reindeer!

CHIARA
Luca, shoes.

LUCA
They're on.

CHIARA
They're wet.

LUCA
It's raining!

OLD MAN
(to Luca)
Come here. Give me the reindeer.

Luca hands it over. The old man turns it, examines it with great seriousness.

OLD MAN (CONT'D)
This reindeer has one antler.

LUCA
It's a one-antler reindeer.

OLD MAN
Then he must be very brave.

Luca grins. A voice carries from the hatch.

NONNA SABINA, 80s, in a black headscarf, climbs down slowly, a tray of pastries held out in front of her.

SABINA
Enzo! You are in my kitchen!

ENZO
(the Old Man, caught)
Sabina, it is a houseboat. It is no one's kitchen.

SABINA
It is mine when I say it is.

CHIARA
Mamma, he's only supervising.

SABINA
He is always supervising. Forty years of supervising.

ENZO
And forty years you ate well.

SABINA
I ate when I cooked.

Enzo laughs. She sets the tray down. She puts her hand to his cheek, briefly, and he turns his face into her palm.

CHIARA
(to Luca, softly)
Go see if Zio Tomaso has docked.

LUCA
Can I take the reindeer?

CHIARA
Take it.

Luca runs out. Enzo and Sabina stand very still.

ENZO
It's been a good year, Sabina.

SABINA
It has.

ENZO
The doctor says --

SABINA
I know what the doctor says.

ENZO
I'd like to be at the table.

SABINA
You will be.

ENZO
I'd like to carve.

SABINA
You'll supervise.

Chiara, who has been pretending not to listen, wipes her eyes with the back of her wrist.

SABINA (CONT'D)
(without turning)
And you. Don't cry in my sauce.

CHIARA
Mamma, I wasn't --

SABINA
Cry on the pasta. It needs the salt.

ENZO
(simultaneously)
Cry on the pasta.

^SABINA
Cry on the pasta.

Chiara laughs, wet and sudden. The boat sways. The lanterns bob. Outside, someone starts to sing.
